By default, when you call launch or async, the coroutine is scheduled to execute immediately. CoroutineStart.LAZY changes this behavior, creating the coroutine in a paused ("New") state.The coroutine will sit quietly in memory and will not run until you explicitly give it a command to start. The most common way to trigger it manually is by calling job.start().
There are 2 Ways to Trigger a Lazy Coroutine once a coroutine is marked lazy because it remains inactive until it receives one of two triggers:
1. job.start(): Starts the coroutine concurrently in the background and returns true immediately. It does not pause the code that called it.
2. job.join() (or .await() for async): Starts the coroutine but suspends the calling line until the coroutine finishes its work completely.
